00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  covid19 has caused a surge in global  unemployment like we've never seen  before  but in the u.s jobs have disappeared  faster than  in any other developed country  and then in may america's unemployment  rate began to rebound  while in other developed nations it  remained relatively stable  the us also creates a ton of jobs  because it's easier to  fire people businesses are more  confident to hire people  so you'd expect the flexible labor  markets to  to move more i suppose in response to  changes in in demand in may and june  the us added about 7.5 million jobs but  the unemployment rate is still at the  highest level since the depression  as the pandemic has put us in uncharted  territory we wanted to know  will american jobs bounce back faster  than the rest of the world  [Music]  it's important to understand what  difference a flexible labor market can  make  in europe there's more of a sense that  the the company has to have a very  strong reason  to be able to fire someone whereas in  the us i think it's much more of a  normal  part of business a flexible labor market  has its pros and cons  and proponents say in normal times it  helps keep unemployment low  by making it easy for people to move  between jobs  just look at the difference in how long  people stay at their jobs in the u.s  versus germany  yeah it's a different mentality in  germany it's not a job for life but the  average  employment tenure is 10 years at a  single company compared with 4.2 i think  it is in the  in the us after the 2008 financial  crisis  the u.s unemployment rate recovered  faster than europe's  which also went through a debt crisis so  it makes sense that a more flexible  labor market would see a bigger jump in  unemployment during a recession  since it's easier for companies to lay  off employees when demand falls  but if you look at what happened at the  start of the pandemic you'll see two  very different outcomes  for two job markets economists deem  flexible  the us saw unemployment rise roughly 11  percentage points from january to april  while japan's unemployment rate grew  half a percentage point  that's where different pandemic  strategies come in  in short the u.s chose to give money  directly to workers while the economy  reboots  while others like japan and europe opted  to subsidize companies and protect  jobs canada and the us i think have been  among the ones that have  seen the biggest jump the main reason i  suppose is because they've  they've tried to protect workers rather  than jobs they haven't  gone for these job furlough schemes in  in  on a grand scale as the europeans  have as part of its first stimulus  package  lawmakers sent americans a twelve  hundred dollar one-time stimulus check  and added six hundred dollars a week to  unemployment benefits  effectively accepting a wave of job  losses  they also funded key industries like  aviation to try and prevent or delay  some layoffs  meanwhile japan's government allocated  nearly 700 billion dollars to business  financing  and enacted a program to subsidize up to  around 140  a day in wages for furloughed employees  the eu has invested roughly 120 billion  dollars in similar short-term work  schemes  since those employees are still on the  books even if they're not working  they're not counted as unemployed that  works well for  those lucky enough to have a permanent  job but  it comes at the cost of very high  unemployment for young people in some  countries  or those on temporary contracts i speak  to people  furlough and um yeah i mean  it's essentially a holiday obviously  there's nervousness there's  concern about the the pandemic but  people are spending their time doing  sports or meeting with friends  it's a very different experience being  you know without work in europe  at the moment than in the u.s but the  question is  how long can it last the labor cost is  only part of  what companies are paying you still have  to pay for the premises and there's  other  fees that you have to pay and you've got  no income so at some point you'll still  have to pull the plug and let those  employees go  that breaking point is going to depend  on how fast economies can recover  the longer the pandemic drags on the  more companies could go bust  erasing jobs that likely won't be  immediately replaced  in june the imf projected the global  economy to grow 5.4  next year but said recovery is still  uncertain  in the next two or three years we'll see  which response was better but  traditionally the us  labor market has been much more dynamic  in kind of reallocating  to growth industries just look at how  the us stock market has outperformed  japan and europe  some big american companies have done  really well throughout the pandemic  and there are high hopes that the  companies that have done well will  decide to create  jobs while europe and japan have kept  unemployment rates relatively stable so  far  they risk supporting jobs that might not  be as valuable by the time the pandemic  ends think aviation or german auto  workers producing parts for engines that  will likely be  phased out will you want to have exactly  the same jobs in place  two years down the line probably not  germany recently allocated 12 billion  dollars to expand its job support  program  until the end of 2021 but the future of  u.s  business support is still unclear as  lawmakers have to regularly assess and  approve stimulus  packages it's just so much uncertainty  about how long this is going to last  the next big debate is going to be about  the exit strategy and how to  how to phase out some of these programs  even if the u.s unemployment rate keeps  falling  the cbo predicts it'll take more than a  decade to reach pre-pandemic levels  while other developed countries haven't  made such long-term projections yet  the ecb forecasts that unemployment in  the eurozone will still be above  pre-pandemic levels in 2022  a full economic recovery from this  pandemic is going to take  years and by the time it's all over we  may be looking at very different labor  markets around the world

